      "problem": "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-2)}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$",
